{"schema": "workere-benchmark-suite/v1", "protocol": "workere-historical-relations/v1", "config": {"seed": 42, "active_dataset": "causal_timebank", "datasets": {"causal_timebank": {"name": "CausalTimeBank-standard-eci", "repo_id": "Nofing/CausalTimeBank-standard-eci-workere", "split": "train", "text_field": "text", "ann_field": "annots", "max_examples": 0, "kfold": {"enabled": true, "n_folds": 10}, "constrain_to_pair_list": false}}, "data": {"shuffle_docs": true, "shuffle_pair_list": true, "reshuffle_per_resample": true, "binary_undirected": false, "vote_to_binary": false, "binary_default": "norel", "novote_norel": true, "labels": ["causes", "causedby", "norel"]}, "experiment": {"resampling": {"enabled": true, "n_runs": 7, "tie_breaking": "norel", "distinct_fewshots": true}}}, "documents": [{"id": "NYT19980206.0460", "idx": 27, "text": "WASHINGTON _ The economy <ei1 created> jobs at a surprisingly robust pace in January , the government <ei3 reported> on Friday , evidence that America 's economic stamina has <ei5 withstood> any <ei6 disruptions> <ei7 caused> so far by the financial <ei8 tumult> in Asia . The Bureau of Labor Statistics <ei9 said> the economy <ei10 added> 358,000 jobs last month , far above the 235,000 <ei12 forecast> by economists . It was the third consecutive month that job generation has been exceptionally strong . With growing opportunities for work <ei13 drawing> more people into the labor force , the unemployment rate <ei14 remained> at 4.7 percent last month , just a notch above its quarter-century low . The <ei16 demand> for workers also <ei17 led> employers to <ei18 raise> wages again last month . Average hourly earnings of production and nonsupervisory employees <ei21 rose> 4 cents , to $ 12.51 . The gain <ei22 left> wages 3.8 percent higher than a year earlier , <ei24 extending> a <ei25 trend> that has <ei26 given> back to workers some of the earning power they <ei27 lost> to inflation in the last decade . But economists <ei29 said> the wage <ei30 increase> was not enough to <ei31 raise> any concerns about higher inflation . And because the Federal Reserve has <ei33 made> clear that it <ei34 expects> the economy to <ei35 slow> in coming months as ripple effects from Asia <ei38 reach> the United States , investors <ei39 disregarded> the large jobs gains , <ei40 betting> that neither a <ei41 rise> in interest rates nor resurgent <ei42 inflation> is <ei43 looming> . Stocks <ei44 rose> , <ei45 pushing> the Dow Jones industrial average up 72.24 points , to 8,189.49 , <ei46 leaving> the index within 70 points of its record high set on Aug . 6 . In the bond market , a sensitive barometer of inflation anxiety , prices <ei48 edged> up , <ei49 pushing> down the yield on the benchmark 30-year Treasury bond to 5.92 percent from 5.93 percent . \" Job <ei51 creation> is sturdy and we have effective price stability , \" <ei52 said> Paul McCulley , an economist at UBS Securities . \" The bottom line is that we 've got a lovely economy . \" With potential inflation <ei54 counterbalanced> by worries about possible price <ei55 declines> , the Fed and its chairman , Alan Greenspan , have <ei56 suggested> that they <ei57 intend> to <ei58 keep> interest rates unchanged until the economy <ei59 tilts> clearly in one direction or another . The job figures for January <ei61 extended> a <ei62 surge> in employment <ei63 gains> that <ei64 began> last fall , just when the labor market had <ei66 showed> signs of cooling . After <ei67 accounting> for a small downward <ei68 revision> Friday to December 's figures , the economy has been <ei71 creating> jobs at a rate of 358,000 a month for the last four months _ and 381,000 over the last three months _ after <ei74 averaging> 242,000 for the first nine months of 1997 . The surge in jobs <ei77 reflects> a remarkable confluence of positive and self-reinforcing economic forces . Inflation has been almost nonexistent , and consumer confidence has been extremely strong . After a short , sharp <ei78 drop> when Asia 's financial problems first <ei79 became> clear , the stock market has <ei80 rebounded> and corporate profits have <ei81 remained> healthy . Long-term interest rates , an important indicator of economic stability , have <ei82 fallen> in the last few months . Lower rates have <ei84 helped> <ei85 invigorate> housing and other interest-sensitive sectors of the economy by <ei86 making> loans more affordable , and have <ei87 allowed> companies to <ei88 continue> <ei89 investing> heavily in more efficient equipment . The likelihood that the federal budget will soon <ei90 move> from deficit into surplus has further <ei91 improved> the outlook . The less money the government needs to borrow , the more is freed for private investment . President Clinton <ei92 welcomed> the job figures at a news <ei93 conference> Friday . His advisers <ei95 said> the <ei96 results> <ei97 reflected> not just from <ei99 balancing> the budget , but also <ei100 initiatives> like improved access to education and training and the opening of foreign markets to trade . \" There are enormous economic and social benefits from our nation 's high-employment economy , \" said Janet Yellen , the chairwoman of the White House 's Council of Economic Advisers . Some of the biggest employment gains <ei101 came> in the construction industry , which <ei102 added> 92,000 jobs in January . The <ei104 increase> <ei105 reflected> the <ei106 strength> of the housing industry , which has been <ei107 helped> not just by declining interest rates but also by unusually warm weather . Service industries also <ei108 showed> solid job gains , as did manufacturers , two areas <ei109 expected> to be hardest <ei110 hit> when the effects of the Asian crisis <ei111 hit> the American economy . The major <ei112 harm> from Asia is likely to come from the <ei113 plunge> in the value of many Asian currencies relative to the dollar , a <ei114 situation> that is <ei115 expected> to <ei116 lead> to a <ei117 surge> of inexpensive imports into the United States , <ei118 hurting> American competitors . And the <ei119 weakness> of the Asian economies is also likely to <ei120 lead> to decreased <ei121 demand> for American exports . \" The economy has been <ei122 running> on the fumes of the strength from before the Asia shock , \" McCulley <ei123 said> . \" Those fumes will <ei124 exhaust> themselves , and the manufacturing sector is going to <ei125 start> getting <ei126 beat> up in the spring . \" The figures <ei127 showed> the unemployment rate for adult men <ei128 declined> to 3.8 percent last month from 4.1 percent in December , the lowest figure for almost two decades . The rate for adult women <ei132 rose> 0.4 percent , to 4.4 percent . For the first time , the Labor Department <ei133 broke> out unemployment by education level as well as gender , age and race . The analysis <ei134 showed> unemployment rates <ei135 dropped> steadily with higher levels of education . Among people with less than a high school education , the rate in January was 7.2 percent . Among high school graduates , the rate was 3.9 percent , and college graduates , 1.9 percent .", "gold": [["ei8", "causes", "ei6"], ["ei16", "causes", "ei18"], ["ei33", "causes", "ei39"], ["ei38", "causes", "ei35"], ["ei54", "causes", "ei57"], ["ei99", "causes", "ei96"], ["ei100", "causes", "ei96"], ["ei106", "causes", "ei104"], ["ei114", "causes", "ei117"], ["ei119", "causes", "ei121"], ["ei6", "causedby", "ei8"], ["ei18", "causedby", "ei16"], ["ei39", "causedby", "ei33"], ["ei35", "causedby", "ei38"], ["ei57", "causedby", "ei54"], ["ei96", "causedby", "ei99"], ["ei96", "causedby", "ei100"], ["ei104", "causedby", "ei106"], ["ei117", "causedby", "ei114"], ["ei121", "causedby", "ei119"]], "lang": "eng", "mentions": ["ei1", "ei3", "ei5", "ei6", "ei7", "ei8", "ei9", "ei10", "ei12", "ei13", "ei14", "ei16", "ei17", "ei18", "ei21", "ei22", "ei24", "ei25", "ei26", "ei27", "ei29", "ei30", "ei31", "ei33", "ei34", "ei35", "ei38", 